1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,885
Pep Guardiola called him a scoring machine.

2
00:00:03,185 --> 00:00:04,981
Jürgen Klopp called him a real beast.

3
00:00:05,281 --> 00:00:07,702
Roy Keane said he looked almost like a League Two player.

4
00:00:08,002 --> 00:00:11,348
So which one is it? To find out, start with one night in July.

5
00:00:11,798 --> 00:00:16,567
July 5th, 2026. A World Cup knockout game. Norway against Brazil.

6
00:00:16,867 --> 00:00:21,033
79 minutes gone. He scores. 90 on the clock, and he scores again.

7
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:25,034
Brazil are out. Norway are in their first ever World Cup quarter-final.

8
00:00:25,334 --> 00:00:28,835
His name is Erling Haaland. And even football's greatest legends still can't explain him.

9
00:00:31,285 --> 00:00:36,445
But the story doesn't start in Norway. It starts in Leeds, England. July 21st, 2000.

10
00:00:36,745 --> 00:00:42,015
His dad, Alf-Inge Haaland, played in the Premier League, for Leeds and for Manchester City. Remember that.

11
00:00:42,315 --> 00:00:47,356
Soon after, the family moved back to Bryne, a small town on Norway's west coast. That's where he learned the game.

12
00:00:48,206 --> 00:00:54,359
Fast forward to May 2019. The Under-20 World Cup in Poland. Norway against Honduras.

13
00:00:54,659 --> 00:01:00,762
He scores nine. In one game. Before that night, nobody at the tournament had scored more than six in a match.

14
00:01:01,062 --> 00:01:05,632
Four months later: his first Champions League game, for Red Bull Salzburg. Hat-trick.

15
00:01:05,932 --> 00:01:09,307
January 2020. Borussia Dortmund came calling.

16
00:01:09,607 --> 00:01:13,561
His debut? He came off the bench and scored a hat-trick in about twenty minutes.

17
00:01:13,861 --> 00:01:17,410
By the time he left: 86 goals in 89 games.

18
00:01:17,710 --> 00:01:22,120
Every big club in Europe wanted him. In 2022, he chose his father's old club.

19
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:29,173
Even rivals were worried. Jürgen Klopp called him a real beast. And, unfortunately for Liverpool, a really good signing.

20
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:34,513
He was right. In his first season, Haaland scored 36 Premier League goals. A new record.

21
00:01:34,813 --> 00:01:39,212
The old record? 34, held by Alan Shearer and Andy Cole. In seasons with 42 games.

22
00:01:39,512 --> 00:01:42,320
Pep Guardiola gave him a nickname: the scoring machine.

23
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:45,619
And that same season, Manchester City won the Treble.

24
00:01:45,919 --> 00:01:48,555
But here's the thing. Not everyone was convinced.

25
00:01:48,855 --> 00:01:53,745
In 2024, Roy Keane said that in general play, Haaland looked almost like a League Two player.

26
00:01:54,045 --> 00:01:57,032
Haaland's reply was short: "I really don't care about that man."

27
00:01:57,882 --> 00:02:03,424
Then he answered on the pitch. Premier League goal number 100. In just 111 games.

28
00:02:03,724 --> 00:02:07,906
Alan Shearer, the league's all-time top scorer, needed 124.

29
00:02:08,206 --> 00:02:13,161
Shearer said it himself: if Haaland stays long enough, there's no doubt he will break that record too.

30
00:02:13,461 --> 00:02:16,838
And for Norway, he became the country's all-time top scorer.

31
00:02:17,688 --> 00:02:23,669
But Norway hadn't played at a World Cup since 1998. First, they had to qualify. They won all eight games.

32
00:02:23,969 --> 00:02:27,458
Haaland scored in every single one. 16 goals.

33
00:02:27,758 --> 00:02:33,232
The last one: a 4-1 win over Italy, in Milan. Norway's first World Cup in 28 years.

34
00:02:33,532 --> 00:02:36,359
And at the World Cup itself? He did what he always does.

35
00:02:36,659 --> 00:02:43,124
Two against Iraq. Two against Senegal. The winner against Ivory Coast. Two to knock out Brazil.

36
00:02:43,424 --> 00:02:47,115
England ended the run in the quarter-final. But he went home with seven goals.

37
00:02:47,965 --> 00:02:52,635
And this season? Top of the Premier League scoring charts again. 9 goals in 11 games.

38
00:02:52,935 --> 00:02:58,255
Nine goals in one game at eighteen. A World Cup quarter-final at twenty-five. And he's still only twenty-six.

39
00:02:58,555 --> 00:03:04,543
Machine, beast, or League Two player? Maybe that's why the legends can't explain him. He isn't finished yet.

40
00:03:05,193 --> 00:03:09,912
Who will he become? Tell us in the comments, and subscribe, because next, it's Mbappé.
